\begin_layout Title
General Equilibrium: Homework 1 (2013)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Author
Nathan Smooha
\end_layout

\begin_layout Problem
(
\emph on
Box Diagram
\emph default
) The Edgeworth box diagram is a device to express an economy.
 Answer the following:
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate
Do we need information on each agent's endowment to determine the size of
 the Edgeworth box?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
One point inside the box shows an allocation.
 Does this admit the destruction of commodities?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Could we draw indifference maps and a budget line, and then determine an
 optimal choice of a consumer outside the box?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
The usual box diagram is not a 3-dimensional box but a rectangle.
 In what economic environment can you imagine a 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

real
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 box?
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
\bar under
Part a.

\series default
\bar default
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To determine the size of the Edgeworth box, we do not require information
 on each agent's endowment.
 The only thing we need to know is the total amount of goods endowed to
 the economy, 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{w}$
\end_inset

.
 We need information on each agent's endowment when we wish to identify
 
\begin_inset Formula $w$
\end_inset

 within the box, which determines each agent's initial endowment of each
 good.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
\bar under
Part b.

\series default
\bar default
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
No, the point inside the box does not admit the destruction of commodities.
 Instead, the point identifies the amount of each agent's initial endowment
 of each good.
 For example, consider the two agent, two commodity case.
 The horizontal distance of the point to the left border identifies agent
 1's endowment of good 1, while the vertical distance of the point to the
 bottom border signifies his endowment of good 2.
 The horizontal distance of the point to the right border identifies agent
 2's endowment of good 1, while the vertical distance of the point to the
 top border signfies his endowment of good 2.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
\bar under
Part c.

\series default
\bar default
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Yes, we could draw indifference maps and a budget line, which can determine
 an optimal choice of a consumer that lies outside the box.
 However, anything that lies outside the Edgeworth box is not feasible,
 so the consumer will choose a point in the box that brings him closest
 to his optimal choice.
 Usually, this will lead to a border solution.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
\bar under
Part d.

\series default
\bar default
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The economic environment which I can imagine a 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

real
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 box would be a pure exchange economy with two agents and three commodities.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Problem
(
\emph on
Lexicographic ordering and existence
\emph default
).
 Consider a pure exchange economy with two persons and two commodities.
 Let each consumer's initial endowment be 
\begin_inset Formula $w_{i}=(1,\frac{1}{2})$
\end_inset

 for 
\begin_inset Formula $i=1,2$
\end_inset

.
 Each consumer's preferences are lexicographic.
 Suppose the first commodity has the first priority for each consumer.
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate
Is this preference an ordering? Justify your answer.
 If your answer is positive, can it be represented by a utility function?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Under what weakest condition you know can a general preference ordering
 be represented by a utility function?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Find the demand function for each consumer of this economy.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Draw each consumer's offer curve in the Edgeworth box diagram.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Find the competitive equilibrium if any.
 If not, consider the reason why.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
\bar under
Part a.

\series default
\bar default
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $X\equiv\mathbb{R}^{2}$
\end_inset

 and define 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim_{lex}$
\end_inset

 as follows: 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ x\equiv(x_{1},x_{2})\in\mathbb{R}^{2},\ \forall\ x^{\prime}\equiv(x_{1}^{\prime},x_{2}^{\prime})\in\mathbb{R}^{2},\ x\succsim_{lex}x^{\prime}\iff x_{1}>x_{1}^{\prime}\ \vee\ [x_{1}=x_{1}^{\prime}\ \wedge\ x_{1}\geq x_{1}^{\prime}]$
\end_inset

.
 To show 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim_{lex}$
\end_inset

 is a preference ordering, we must show that it is reflexive, complete,
 and transitive.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\emph on
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Reflexive
\emph default
: Take 
\begin_inset Formula $x\in X$
\end_inset

.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $\{x_{1},x_{2}\}\subseteq\mathbb{R}$
\end_inset

, then 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{1}\geq x_{1}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{2}\geq x_{2}$
\end_inset

.
 Thus, 
\begin_inset Formula $x\succsim_{lex}x$
\end_inset

.
 Therefore, 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim_{lex}$
\end_inset

 is reflexive.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\emph on
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Complete
\emph default
: Take 
\begin_inset Formula $\{x,x^{\prime}\}\subseteq X$
\end_inset

.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $\{x_{1},x_{1}^{\prime},x_{2},x_{2}^{\prime}\}\subseteq\mathbb{R}$
\end_inset

, then 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{1}\geq x_{1}^{\prime}\ \vee\ x_{1}^{\prime}\geq x_{1}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{2}\geq x_{2}^{\prime}\ \vee\ x_{2}^{\prime}\geq x_{2}$
\end_inset

.
 So, 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{1}>x_{1}^{\prime}\ \vee\ x_{1}^{\prime}>x_{1}$
\end_inset

, or 
\begin_inset Formula $\left[x_{1}=x_{1}^{\prime}\ \wedge\ \left(x_{2}\geq x_{2}^{\prime}\ \vee\ x_{2}^{\prime}\geq x_{2}\right)\right]$
\end_inset

.
 Thus, 
\begin_inset Formula $x\succsim_{lex}x^{\prime}$
\end_inset

 or 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{\prime}\succsim_{lex}x$
\end_inset

.
 Therefore, 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim_{lex}$
\end_inset

 is complete.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\emph on
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Transitive
\emph default
: Take 
\begin_inset Formula $\{x,x^{\prime},x^{\prime\prime}\}\subseteq X$
\end_inset

.
 Assume 
\begin_inset Formula $x\succsim_{lex}x^{\prime}\ \wedge\ x^{\prime}\succsim_{lex}x^{\prime\prime}$
\end_inset

.
 Then, 
\begin_inset Formula $[x_{1}>x_{1}^{\prime}\ \vee\ \left(x_{1}=x_{1}^{\prime}\ \wedge\ x_{2}\geq x_{2}^{\prime}\right)]$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\left[x_{1}^{\prime}>x_{1}^{\prime\prime}\ \vee\ \left(x_{1}^{\prime}=x_{1}^{\prime\prime}\ \wedge\ x_{2}^{\prime}\geq x_{2}^{\prime\prime}\right)\right]$
\end_inset

.
 This is logically equivalent to 
\begin_inset Formula $\left[x_{1}>x_{1}^{\prime}\ \wedge\ x_{1}^{\prime}>x_{1}^{\prime\prime}\right]$
\end_inset


\family roman
