\begin_layout Standard
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $p\equiv\frac{p_{2}}{p_{1}}$
\end_inset

.
 Since each agent's preferences are monotone, then they are locally non-satiatie
d.
 So, the budget constraint holds with equality for both consumers, and thus,
 Walras's Law holds.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\emph on
\bar under
Case:
\emph default
\bar default
 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{1}>0$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{2}>0$
\end_inset

.
 The maximization problem for agent 1 is:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\max & u_{1}=\min\{x_{11},4(x_{12}-3)\}\\
\mbox{s.t.} & x_{11}+px_{12}=2+2p
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Using the budget constraint, we get a formula for 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{11}$
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\[
x_{11}=2+2p-px_{12}
\]

\end_inset

We plug this equation into the utility function and solve for 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{12}$
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
2+2p-px_{12} & = & 4x_{12}-12\\
(4+p)x_{12} & = & 14+2p\\
x_{12}(p) & = & \frac{2(p+7)}{p+4}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

