, then a competitive equilibrium exists.
 Also, 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{1}>0$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{2}=0$
\end_inset

.
 Thus, multiple equilibria can exist in this case.
 The competitive equilibria are given by:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
x^{*}=[x_{1}^{*},x_{2}^{*}] & = & \left[\left(\begin{array}{c}
2\\
t
\end{array}\right),\left(\begin{array}{c}
2\\
4-t
\end{array}\right):t\in\left[\frac{7}{2},4\right]\right]\\
p^{*} & = & \left(\begin{array}{c}
p_{1}\\
0
\end{array}\right)
