\begin_layout Problem
Consider the following (private ownership) perfectly competitive economy.
 There are two consumer with utility funcitons 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{1}=(x_{11})^{\alpha}(x_{21})^{1-\alpha}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{2}=(x_{21})^{\beta}(x_{22})^{1-\beta}$
\end_inset

, respectively, where 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{1}$
\end_inset

 represents leisure and 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{2}$
\end_inset

 represents consumption and 
\begin_inset Formula $0<\alpha<1$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $0<\beta<1$
\end_inset

.
 There is also a single firm that has a production function 
\begin_inset Formula $q=z^{\frac{1}{2}}$
\end_inset

, where 
\begin_inset Formula $z$
\end_inset

 is labor input and 
\begin_inset Formula $q$
\end_inset

 is output of good 2.
 the aggregate endowment of leisure equals 
\begin_inset Formula $L>0$
\end_inset

.
 Each consumer owns a fraction of the aggregate endowment and the firm.
 Consumer 1's share of each is 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta$
\end_inset

, and consumer 2's share of each is 
\begin_inset Formula $1-\theta$
\end_inset

, where 
\begin_inset Formula $0<\theta<1$
\end_inset

.
 (The economy's initial endowment of good 2 is zero; this good has to be
 produced.)
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate
Show that consumer demand in this economy is equivalent to that there were
 a single consumer with 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

aggregate preferences.
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 Write down this consumer's preferences.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Using this fact or otherwise, compute the Walrasian equilibrium for this
 economy.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
\bar under
Part a.

\series default
\bar default
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $p\equiv\frac{p_{2}}{p_{1}}$
\end_inset

.
 Since each agent's preferences are monotone, then they are locally non-satiatie
d.
 So, the budget constraint holds with equality for both consumers, and thus,
 Walras's Law holds.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
First, let us maximize the firm's profit
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\max\ \Pi & = & pz^{\frac{1}{2}}-z\\
\frac{\partial\Pi}{\partial z} & = & \frac{p}{2}z^{-\frac{1}{2}}-1\\
\frac{p}{2}z^{-\frac{1}{2}}-1 & = & 0\\
z & = & \frac{p^{2}}{4}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Thus, the firm's optimal production plan is
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
y(p)=\left(\begin{array}{c}
-\frac{p^{2}}{4}\\
\frac{p^{2}}{2}
\end{array}\right)
\]

\end_inset

and its maximum profit is 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\Pi^{*}=p\left(\frac{p^{2}}{4}\right)^{\frac{1}{2}}-\frac{p^{2}}{4}=\frac{p^{2}}{2}-\frac{p^{2}}{4}=\frac{p^{2}}{4}
\]

\end_inset

Since both utility funcitons are Cobb-Douglas, then the demand functions
 for each agent have the following form:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
x_{1}(p) & = & \left(\begin{array}{c}
\alpha\theta L+\alpha\theta\frac{p^{2}}{4}\\
\frac{(1-\alpha)\theta L}{p}+\frac{(1-\alpha)\theta p}{4}
\end{array}\right)\\
x_{2}(p) & = & \left(\begin{array}{c}
\beta(1-\theta)L+\beta(1-\theta)\frac{p^{2}}{4}\\
\frac{(1-\beta)(1-\theta)L}{p}+\frac{(1-\beta)(1-\theta)p}{4}
\end{array}\right)
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

So, the aggregate demand function is
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\hat{x}(p) & = & \left[\begin{array}{c}
\alpha\theta L+\alpha\theta\frac{p^{2}}{4}+\beta(1-\theta)L+\beta(1-\theta)\frac{p^{2}}{4}\\
\frac{(1-\alpha)\theta L}{p}+\frac{(1-\alpha)\theta p}{4}+\frac{(1-\beta)(1-\theta)L}{p}+\frac{(1-\beta)(1-\theta)p}{4}
\end{array}\right]\\
 & = & \left[\begin{array}{c}
\left(\alpha\theta+\beta(1-\theta)\right)L+\left(\alpha\theta+\beta(1-\theta)\right)\frac{p^{2}}{4}\\
\left((1-\alpha)\theta+(1-\beta)(1-\theta)\right)\frac{L}{p}+\left((1-\alpha)\theta+(1-\beta)(1-\theta)\right)\frac{p}{4}
\end{array}\right]
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Now, we can show that this aggregate demand function is equivalent to having
 a single consumer with 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

aggregate preferences.
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\gamma=\alpha\theta+\beta(1-\theta)$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $(1-\gamma)=(1-\alpha)\theta+(1-\beta)(1-\theta)$
\end_inset

.
 We will now show that 
\begin_inset Formula $\gamma+(1-\gamma)=1$
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\gamma+(1-\gamma) & = & \left[\alpha\theta+\beta(1-\theta)\right]+\left[(1-\alpha)\theta+(1-\beta)(1-\theta)\right]\\
 & = & \alpha\theta+\beta-\beta\theta+\theta-\alpha\theta+1-\theta-\beta+\beta\theta\\
 & = & 1
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Since 
\begin_inset Formula $0<\alpha<1$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $0<\beta<1$
\end_inset

, then 
\begin_inset Formula $0<\gamma<1$
\end_inset

.
 Thus, the aggregate demand function can be rewritten as
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\hat{x}(p)=\left[\begin{array}{c}
\gamma L+\gamma\frac{p^{2}}{4}\\
(1-\gamma)\frac{L}{p}+(1-\gamma)\frac{p}{4}
\end{array}\right]
\]

\end_inset

This function can also be obtained if we maximize the utility function of
 a representative consumer
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\max & u(x_{A1},x_{A2})=x_{A1}^{\gamma}x_{A2}^{1-\gamma}\\
\mbox{s.t.} & x_{A1}+px_{A2}=L+\frac{p^{2}}{4}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
\bar under
Part b.

\series default
\bar default
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To find the competitive equilibrium, we must find all 
\begin_inset Formula $p\in\mathbb{R}_{+}^{2}$
\end_inset

 such that 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{z}(p)=\hat{x}(p)-\hat{w}=0$
\end_inset

.
 Important to note, 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{w}=(L,0)$
\end_inset

.
 Also, by Walras' Law, it is enough to show only one market clearing for
 
\begin_inset Formula $L=2$
\end_inset

.
 Let us consider the market for leisure.
 By the market clearing condition, we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\hat{x}_{1}(p)-\hat{y}_{1}(p) & = & L\\
\left(\gamma L+\gamma\frac{p^{2}}{4}\right)-\left(-\frac{p^{2}}{4}\right) & = & L\\
(1+\gamma)\frac{p^{2}}{4} & = & L\\
p^{2} & = & 4\frac{(1-\gamma)L}{(1+\gamma)}\\
p^{*} & = & 2\sqrt{\frac{L(1-\gamma)}{(1+\gamma)}}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Since 
\begin_inset Formula $0<\gamma<1$
\end_inset

, then 
\begin_inset Formula $(1-\gamma)>0$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $(1+\gamma)>0$
\end_inset

, so 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{*}$
\end_inset

 is always well defined.
 Thus, a competitive equilibrium is given by:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
x^{*}=\left[x_{1}^{*},x_{2}^{*}\right] & = & \left[\left(\begin{array}{c}
\alpha\theta L\left[1+\frac{(1-\gamma)}{(1+\gamma)}\right]\\
\frac{(1-\alpha)\theta}{2}\left[L\sqrt{\frac{(1+\gamma)}{L(1-\gamma)}}+\sqrt{\frac{L(1-\gamma)}{(1+\gamma)}}\right]
\end{array}\right),\left(\begin{array}{c}
\beta(1-\theta)L\left[1+\frac{(1-\gamma)}{(1+\gamma)}\right]\\
\frac{(1-\beta)(1-\theta)}{2}\left[L\sqrt{\frac{(1+\gamma)}{L(1-\gamma)}}+\sqrt{\frac{L(1-\gamma)}{(1+\gamma)}}\right]
\end{array}\right)\right]\\
y^{*} & = & \left(\begin{array}{c}
-\sqrt{\frac{L(1-\gamma)}{(1+\gamma)}}\\
2\sqrt{\frac{L(1-\gamma)}{(1+\gamma)}}
\end{array}\right)\\
p^{*} & = & \left(\begin{array}{c}
1\\
2\sqrt{\frac{L(1-\gamma)}{(1+\gamma)}}
\end{array}\right)
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Problem
(
\emph on
