\bar under
Part a.

\series default
\bar default
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To see if a competitive equilibrium exists, we must first maximize the utility
 functions of each agent to find their respective demand functions, and
 then we must check the market clearing condition.
 Since each agent's preferences are monotone, then they are locally non-satiatie
d.
 So, the budget constraint holds with equality for both consumers, and thus,
 Walras's Law holds.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Utility maximizaiton for agent 1 subject to his budget constraint:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\max\  & u_{1}(x_{11},x_{12},x_{13})=x_{11}(x_{13})^{2}\\
\mbox{s.t.} & p_{1}x_{11}+p_{3}x_{13}=3p_{1}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

So, setting up the Lagrangian and deriving the first order conditions, we
 get:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
L & = & x_{11}x_{13}^{2}+\lambda(3p_{1}-p_{1}x_{11}-p_{3}x_{13})\\
\frac{\partial L}{\partial x_{11}}:\ x_{13}^{2}-\lambda p_{1}=0 & \iff & \lambda=\frac{x_{13}^{2}}{p_{1}}\\
\frac{\partial L}{\partial x_{13}}:\ 2x_{11}x_{13}-\lambda p_{3}=0 & \iff & \lambda=\frac{2x_{11}x_{13}}{p_{3}}\\
\frac{x_{13}^{2}}{p_{1}} & = & \frac{2x_{11}x_{13}}{p_{3}}\\
x_{13} & = & \frac{2p_{1}x_{11}}{p_{3}}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

We plug this expression for 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{13}$
\end_inset

 into the budget constraint, and then we find agent 1's demand for each
 good:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
p_{1}x_{11}+p_{3}\left(\frac{2p_{1}x_{11}}{p_{3}}\right) & = & 3p_{1}\\
p_{1}x_{11}+2p_{1}x_{11} & = & 3p_{1}\\
x_{11} & = & 1\\
x_{13}(p) & = & 2\frac{p_{1}}{p_{3}}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Thus, agent 1's demand function is given by:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
x_{1}(p)=\left(\begin{array}{c}
1\\
0\\
2\frac{p_{1}}{p_{3}}
\end{array}\right)
\]

\end_inset

Utility maximization for agent 2 subject to his budget constraint:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\max & u_{2}(x_{21},x_{22},x_{23})=3(x_{21})^{\frac{1}{2}}+2(x_{23})\\
\mbox{s.t.} & p_{1}x_{21}+p_{3}x_{23}=3p_{1}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

So, setting up the Lagrangian and deriving the first order conditions, we
 get:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
L & = & 3(x_{21})^{\frac{1}{2}}+2(x_{23})+\lambda\left(3p_{1}-p_{1}x_{21}-p_{3}x_{23}\right)\\
\frac{\partial L}{\partial x_{21}}:\ \frac{3}{2}(x_{21})^{-\frac{1}{2}}-\lambda p_{1}=0 & \iff & \lambda=\frac{3}{2}(x_{21})^{-\frac{1}{2}}p_{1}^{-1}\\
\frac{\partial L}{\partial x_{23}}:\ 2-\lambda p_{3}=0 & \iff & \lambda=\frac{2}{p_{3}}\\
\frac{3}{2}(x_{21})^{-\frac{1}{2}}p_{1}^{-1} & = & \frac{2}{p_{3}}\\
x_{21}(p) & = & \left(\frac{3}{4}\frac{p_{3}}{p_{1}}\right)^{2}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Now, we plug this function for 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{21}$
\end_inset

 into the budget constraint:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
p_{1}\left(\frac{3}{4}\frac{p_{3}}{p_{1}}\right)^{2}+p_{3}x_{23} & = & 3p_{1}\\
\frac{9}{16}\frac{p_{3}^{2}}{p_{1}}+p_{3}x_{23} & = & 3p_{1}\\
x_{23}(p) & = & 3\frac{p_{1}}{p_{3}}-\frac{9}{16}\frac{p_{3}}{p_{1}}\\
 & = & 3\left(\frac{p_{1}}{p_{3}}-\frac{3}{16}\frac{p_{3}}{p_{1}}\right)\\
x_{23}\geq0 & \iff & \frac{p_{1}}{p_{3}}-\frac{3}{16}\frac{p_{3}}{p_{1}}\geq0\\
 & \iff & \frac{p_{1}}{p_{3}}\geq\frac{\sqrt{3}}{4}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Thus, agent 2's demand function is given by:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
x_{2}(p)=\begin{cases}
\left(\begin{array}{c}
\left(\frac{3}{4}\frac{p_{3}}{p_{1}}\right)^{2}\\
0\\
3\left(\frac{p_{1}}{p_{3}}-\frac{3}{16}\frac{p_{3}}{p_{1}}\right)
\end{array}\right) & \mbox{if}\ \frac{p_{1}}{p_{3}}\geq\frac{\sqrt{3}}{4}\\
\left(\begin{array}{c}
3\\
0\\
0
\end{array}\right) & \mbox{if}\ \frac{p_{1}}{p_{3}}<\frac{\sqrt{3}}{4}
\end{cases}
\]

\end_inset

To find the competitive equilibrium, we must find all 
\begin_inset Formula $p\in\mathbb{R}_{+}^{3}$
\end_inset

 such that 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{z}(p)=\hat{x}(p)-\hat{w}=0$
\end_inset

.
 When 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{p_{1}}{p_{3}}\geq\frac{\sqrt{3}}{4}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{x}_{3}(p)>\hat{w}_{3}$
\end_inset

, and when 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{p_{1}}{p_{3}}<\frac{\sqrt{3}}{4}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{x}_{1}(p)>\hat{w}_{1}$
\end_inset

.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{x}(p)\nleq\hat{w}\ \forall\ p\in\mathbb{R}_{+}^{2}$
\end_inset

, then no competitive equilibrium exists.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
\bar under
Part b.

\series default
\bar default
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
From the problem description, we can see that both agents only wish to consume
 goods 1 and 3.
 On the production side, firm 1 uses good 1 to produce good 2, while firm
 2 uses good 2 to produce good 3.
 Consequently, if none of the firms produce, then we immediately revert
 back to the situation depicted in part a.
 In addition, if only one of the firms produce, then good 3 would not be
 produced in either case, so we would again encounter the same problem from
 part a.
 Thus, both firms must produce for a competitive equilibrium to exist.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Taking this fact into account, we can deduce a few conclusions regarding
 the equilibrium price system.
 Most importantly, firm 2 exhibits constant returns to scale since its productio
n function is linear.
 If 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{p_{2}}{2}>p_{3}$
\end_inset

, then firm 2 would not produce because its profits would be negative if
 it did.
 If 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{3}>\frac{p_{2}}{2}$
\end_inset

, then firm 2 would be able to achieve infinite profit by demanding an infinite
 amount of good 2.
 However, this situation would not lead to a competitive equilibrium since
 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{z}(p)\nleq0$
\end_inset

.
 So, firm 2 must make zero profit for a competitive equilibrium to exist.
 Thus, 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{3}=\frac{p_{2}}{2}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
\bar under
Part c.

\series default
\bar default
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Let us normalize prices so that 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{3}=1$
\end_inset

.
 With this normalization, then 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{2}=2$
\end_inset

 by the argument in part b.
 Finally, define 
\begin_inset Formula $p\equiv\frac{p_{1}}{p_{3}}$
\end_inset

.
 Note, since each agent's preferences are strictly monotone, then they are
 locally non-satiatied, and thus, Walras' Law holds for both consumers.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
First off, let us maximize profits for firm 1:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\max\ \Pi_{1} & = & 2y_{12}-py_{11}\\
 & = & 2(y_{11})^{\frac{1}{2}}-py_{11}\\
\frac{\partial\Pi_{1}}{\partial y_{11}} & = & y_{11}^{-\frac{1}{2}}-p\\
y_{11}^{-\frac{1}{2}}-p & = & 0\\
y_{11}(p) & = & p^{-2}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Thus, firm 1's optimal production plan is given by:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
y_{1}(p)=\left(\begin{array}{c}
-p^{-2}\\
p^{-1}\\
0
\end{array}\right)
\]

\end_inset

and its maximum profit is:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\Pi_{1} & = & 2(p^{-2})^{\frac{1}{2}}-p^{-1}\\
 & = & p^{-1}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Next, from the arguments in part b, firm 2 must produce and 
\begin_inset Formula $\Pi_{2}=0$
\end_inset

 for a competitive equilibrium to exist.
 When this occurs, the firm will use all of the good 2 available to produce
 good 3.
 As a result, firm 2's optimal production plan is given by:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
y_{2}(p)=\left(\begin{array}{c}
0\\
-p^{-1}\\
2p^{-1}
\end{array}\right)
\]

\end_inset

Second, we maximize agent 1's utility subject to his budget constraint:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\max\  & u_{1}(x_{11},x_{12},x_{13})=x_{11}(x_{13})^{2}\\
\mbox{s.t.} & px_{11}+x_{13}=3p+p^{-1}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

So, setting up the Lagrangian and deriving the first order conditions, we
 get:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
L & = & x_{11}(x_{13})^{2}+\lambda(3p+p^{-1}-px_{11}-x_{13})\\
\frac{\partial L}{\partial x_{11}}:\ x_{13}^{2}-\lambda p=0 & \iff & \lambda=\frac{x_{13}^{2}}{p}\\
\frac{\partial L}{\partial x_{13}}:\ 2x_{11}x_{13}-\lambda=0 & \iff & \lambda=2x_{11}x_{13}\\
\frac{x_{13}^{2}}{p} & = & 2x_{11}x_{13}\\
x_{13} & = & 2px_{11}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

We plug this expression for 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{13}$
\end_inset

 into the budget constraint, and then we find agent 1's demand for each
 good:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
3px_{11} & = & 3p+p^{-1}\\
x_{11}(p) & = & 1+\frac{1}{3p^{2}}\\
x_{13}(p) & = & 2p+\frac{2}{3p}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Thus, agent 1's demand function is given by:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
x_{1}(p)=\left(\begin{array}{c}
1+\frac{1}{3p^{2}}\\
0\\
2p+\frac{2}{3p}
\end{array}\right)
\]

\end_inset

Moving on, we agent 2's utility subject to his budget constraint:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\max & u_{2}(x_{21},x_{22},x_{23})=3(x_{21})^{\frac{1}{2}}+2(x_{23})\\
\mbox{s.t.} & px_{21}+px_{23}=3p
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

This is the same maximization problem agent 2 faced in part a.
 Thus, his demand function is given by:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
x_{2}(p)=\begin{cases}
\left(\begin{array}{c}
\left(\frac{3}{4p}\right)^{2}\\
0\\
3\left(p-\frac{3}{16p}\right)
\end{array}\right) & \mbox{if}\ p\geq\frac{\sqrt{3}}{4}\\
\left(\begin{array}{c}
3\\
0\\
0
\end{array}\right) & \mbox{if}\ p<\frac{\sqrt{3}}{4}
\end{cases}
\]

\end_inset

Now, to find the competitive equilibrium, we must find all 
\begin_inset Formula $p\in\mathbb{R}_{+}^{3}$
\end_inset

 such that 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{z}(p)=\hat{x}(p)-\hat{w}-\hat{y}(p)=0$
\end_inset

.
 Important to note, by Walras' Law, it is enough to show only two markets
 clearing for 
\begin_inset Formula $L=3$
\end_inset

.
 We have already confirmed that the market for good 2 clears and 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{2}=2$
\end_inset

.
 So, we only need to show that one more market clears.
 Let us consider the market for good 3.
 The aggregate excess demand function for good 3 is given by:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\hat{z}_{3}(p)=\begin{cases}
5p-\frac{91}{48p} & \mbox{if}\ p\geq\frac{\sqrt{3}}{4}\\
2p-\frac{4}{3p} & \mbox{if}\ p<\frac{\sqrt{3}}{4}
\end{cases}
\]

\end_inset

Suppose 
\begin_inset Formula $p<\frac{\sqrt{3}}{4}$
\end_inset

.
 By the market clearing condition, we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
2p-\frac{4}{3p} & = & 0\\
p^{2} & = & \frac{2}{3}\\
p & = & \sqrt{\frac{2}{3}}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Note, this solution for 
\begin_inset Formula $p$
\end_inset

 does not fall in the required range.
 So, a competitive equilibrium fails to exist in this case.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Suppose 
\begin_inset Formula $p\geq\frac{\sqrt{3}}{4}$
\end_inset

.
 By the market clearing condition, we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
5p-\frac{91}{48p} & = & 0\\
p^{2} & = & \frac{91}{240}\\
p^{*} & = & \sqrt{\frac{91}{240}}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Since 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{*}\geq\frac{\sqrt{3}}{4}$
\end_inset

, then 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{*}$
\end_inset

 constitutes an equilibrium price.
 Thus, the competitive equilibrium is given by:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
x^{*}=\left[x_{1}^{*},x_{2}^{*}\right] & = & \left[\left(\begin{array}{c}
\frac{171}{91}\\
0\\
2\sqrt{\frac{91}{240}}+\frac{2}{3}\sqrt{\frac{240}{91}}
\end{array}\right),\left(\begin{array}{c}
\frac{135}{91}\\
0\\
3\sqrt{\frac{91}{240}}-\frac{3}{16}\sqrt{\frac{240}{91}}
\end{array}\right)\right]\\
y^{*}=[y_{1}^{*},y_{2}^{*}] & = & \left[\left(\begin{array}{c}
-\frac{240}{91}\\
\sqrt{\frac{240}{91}}\\
0
\end{array}\right),\left(\begin{array}{c}
0\\
-\sqrt{\frac{240}{91}}\\
2\sqrt{\frac{240}{91}}
\end{array}\right)\right]\\
p^{*} & = & \left(\begin{array}{c}
\sqrt{\frac{91}{240}}\\
2\\
1
\end{array}\right)
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

